<h3 id="1-centering-the-predictors">1. centering the predictors</h3>
<p>Since the $\mathbb{1}$ column vector inside the design matrix is not affected by centering, we will split $X$ into $\mathbb{1}$ and the matrix of observed predictor values, then center the values in the latter.</p>

<p>[X_c = \begin{bmatrix} 
        \mathbb{1} &amp; X_1
        \end{bmatrix}]</p>

<p>is the centered and augmented design matrix.</p>

<p>Our regression coefficients for the centered form is specified as</p>

<p>[\beta_c = \begin{bmatrix} 
            \alpha <br />
            \beta_1 <br />
            \vdots <br />
            \beta_p
          \end{bmatrix}
        = \begin{bmatrix}
            \alpha <br />
            \boldsymbol{\beta_1}
          \end{bmatrix}]</p>

<p>where</p>

<p>[\boldsymbol{\beta_1} = \begin{bmatrix}
                     \beta_1 <br />
                     \vdots <br />
                     \beta_p
                   \end{bmatrix}]</p>

<p>Then our model becomes 
\(y = X_c \beta_c + \epsilon = \alpha\mathbb{1} +\)
